SUMMARY:Don't Look Now | Friday Night Frights
DESCRIPTION:Adapted from a Daphne du Maurier short story\, Don&#8217\;t L
 ook Now follows John (Donald Sutherland) and Christine (Julie Christie)\,
  still grieving over the accidental death of their daughter\, as they hea
 d to Venice for John's work restoring a church. Soon\, Laura meets two si
 sters who claim to be in touch with the spirit of their dead daughter and
  the couple must decide what is real\, what is supernatural\, and what is
  somewhere in-between. Renowned for innovative editing style\, memorable 
 recurring aesthetic motifs\, an impressionistic formal style\, and nonlin
 ear storytelling that destabilizes the viewer\, the film was controversia
 l on its release but is now considered a classic\, landing multiples time
 s on Sight and Sound's list of the best films of all time\, the BFI list 
 of greatest British films\, and was reevaluated by Roget Ebert as an occu
 lt-horror classic. Don't Look Now has also inspired filmmakers like Danny
  Boyle\, Lynne Ramsey\, Ari Aster\, Steven Soderbergh\, and many more. [1
 10 min\; horror\; English and Italian]
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Adapted from a Daphne du Maurier short 
 story\, <i>Don&#8217\;t Look Now</i> follows John (Donald Sutherland) and
  Christine (Julie Christie)\, still grieving over the accidental death of
  their daughter\, as they head to Venice for John's work restoring a chur
 ch. Soon\, Laura meets two sisters who claim to be in touch with the spir
 it of their dead daughter and the couple must decide what is real\, what 
 is supernatural\, and what is somewhere in-between. Renowned for innovati
 ve editing style\, memorable recurring aesthetic motifs\, an impressionis
 tic formal style\, and nonlinear storytelling that destabilizes the viewe
 r\, the film was controversial on its release but is now considered a cla
 ssic\, landing multiples times on <i>Sight and Sound</i>'s list of the be
 st films of all time\, the BFI list of greatest British films\, and was r
 eevaluated by Roget Ebert as an occult-horror classic. <i>Don't Look Now<
 /i> has also inspired filmmakers like Danny Boyle\, Lynne Ramsey\, Ari As
 ter\, Steven Soderbergh\, and many more. [110 min\; horror\; English and 
 Italian]</p>
